\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Pulling a functional back along a supplied algebra map}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, #{A,B} unital associative
#{R}-algebras, and let #{\iota:B\to A} be a supplied
[unital algebra homomorphism](ca-001A). Precomposition defines an
#{R}-linear map in the opposite direction on ordinary duals:
##{\begin{aligned}
\iota^\flat:\operatorname{Hom}_R(A,R)&\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Hom}_R(B,R),\\
\omega&\longmapsto\omega\circ\iota.
\end{aligned}}
It sends [normalized functionals](fdip-0021) to normalized functionals.
For #{\kappa:C\to B},
##{(\iota\circ\kappa)^\flat=\kappa^\flat\circ\iota^\flat,
\qquad(\mathrm{id}_A)^\flat=\mathrm{id}.}
When #{\iota} is inclusion this is literal restriction; for an injective
map it becomes restriction after identifying #{B} with its image. No
injectivity is needed for precomposition itself. A partition of a set by
itself does not supply an algebra map between arbitrary algebras.}
\proof{
\p{For #{b,c\in B} and #{r,s\in R}, linearity of #{\iota} and
#{\omega} gives
#{(\omega\iota)(rb+sc)=r(\omega\iota)(b)+s(\omega\iota)(c)}.
Pointwise operations on the dual also give
#{(r\omega+s\nu)\iota=r(\omega\iota)+s(\nu\iota)}.
Since #{\iota(1_B)=1_A}, normalization follows from
#{(\omega\iota)(1_B)=\omega(1_A)=1_R}.
For every #{c\in C}, both sides of the composition identity take
#{\omega} to #{\omega(\iota(\kappa(c)))}; the identity law is immediate
by evaluation.}
}
\p{Linearity and preservation of the unit suffice for the normalization
calculation; multiplication preservation additionally respects observable
products. Without unitality, the zero multiplicative linear map between
nonzero real algebras pulls every normalized functional back to zero.}

\p{Over #{K=\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C}, suppose both algebras carry the
star laws in [the state definition](fdip-0024), and suppose additionally
#{\iota(b^*)=\iota(b)^*}. Then #{\iota^\flat} sends algebraic states
to algebraic states.}
\proof{
\p{For a state #{\omega},
#{(\omega\iota)(b^*)=\omega(\iota(b)^*)=\overline{\omega(\iota(b))}}.
Multiplication and star compatibility give
##{(\omega\iota)(b^*b)=\omega(\iota(b)^*\iota(b))\geq0.}
Normalization was proved above. Identities preserve star, and
#{(\iota\kappa)(c^*)=\iota(\kappa(c)^*)=(\iota\kappa(c))^*}, so
composition preserves the same hypotheses.}
}
\p{Star compatibility cannot be omitted. Give #{\mathbb C[z]} the
involution conjugating coefficients and fixing #{z}; give #{\mathbb C}
ordinary conjugation. Evaluation at #{z=i} is a unital complex algebra
map. The identity functional on #{\mathbb C} is a state, but its pullback
takes #{z^*z=z^2} to #{-1}, so is not positive.}

\p{The dual-map construction is supported by Mathlib's
\leanref{LinearMap.dualMap}, \leanref{LinearMap.dualMap_apply},
\leanref{LinearMap.dualMap_id} and \leanref{LinearMap.dualMap_comp_dualMap};
these do not require finite dimension. The star-preserving map structures
\leanref{StarAlgHom} and \leanref{StarAlgHom.comp} concern supplied star
operations; the genuine star laws above remain explicit hypotheses.
For the algebra and positivity conventions see
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks} and
\citet{Definitions 3.7 and 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
The normalization and positivity assertions here are proved directly.}
